# The grow conformance scene: the layout contract as an assertion
# rather than a recording.
#
# column#0 and row#0 are each the scene's ONLY container of their kind,
# which is what makes naming them safe — container targets index by
# creation order and that order legitimately differs per language (see
# tools/check-steps.sh).
#
# Every child of both containers is a grower, so each split is exactly
# weight/Sigma-weight: 1,2,1 divide the column 25/50/25 and the row's
# 1,3 divide its width 25/75, whatever a label or a button happens to
# measure there. Shares and never sizes — a size is a platform metric
# and could not be compared byte-for-byte at all.
#
# The shares are kept clear of every platform's minimum control size
# (the textarea's declared 96pt floor is the binding one, GTK's 34pt
# button height next; the 540x330 desktop window with its 16pt root
# inset leaves column tracks of ~63/126/63), or the scene would measure
# the minimums rather than the contract.
#
# root-fills before the shares: a share is a percentage of the
# children's SUM, which is total-invariant — a mounted root hugging its
# content at a fraction of the window still splits 25/25/50, and that
# blind spot shipped twice (GTK, then UIKit) before this line existed.
#
# The row also carries the spacing prop's conformance exercise: a
# non-default 12-unit gap, set through each language's sugar. fills is
# what gates it — children PLUS GAPS span the content box, so a
# backend that ignores the write (or an observation that hardcodes the
# 8-unit default) misses by 4 units and fails. Shares cannot see
# spacing at all (gaps are excluded from tracks by design).
#
# fills after root-fills: the same blindness one level down, and the
# third instance of the class. A root can be forced full-size by its
# window while the container pools the leftover as slack BESIDE its
# children — growers then hold their weight RATIO at natural size,
# every share still passes, and root-fills sees a full root. AppKit
# shipped exactly that (gravity-areas distribution) until a 540x330
# window made 200pt of slack visible; expect_fills is the gate that
# sees it structurally: the children (plus the normalized 8-unit gaps)
# must span the container's content box on the main axis.
#
# expect_fills on a WIDGET is the other half of the same contract, and
# the half a share cannot state: three of the four backends read a
# share off the TRACK (SwiftUI's cell frame, WinUI's RowDefinition,
# Compose's weighted cell) — on purpose, since a control that hugs
# inside its track is platform flavor no byte-compared assertion could
# carry — so a widget with a HARD-CODED size splits its container
# exactly right and renders wrong. A textarea is the widget it rides
# on because a fixed natural box is exactly that shape: 240x96 frozen
# in the SwiftUI frame and in WinUI's Height gave an editor asking for
# a full-window buffer a small box, with every line below still green.
expect label#0 "grow probe"
expect_root_fills
expect_fills column#0
expect_fills row#0
expect_fills textarea#0
expect_shares column#0 "25,50,25"
expect_shares row#0 "25,75"
